  "construals": [
    {
      "perspective": "speaker",
      "analysis": "The speaker presents love as an existing emotional state and as the reason she welcomes a shared future: she hopes to live with the hearer \"because I love you.\" The wedding context also gives the avowal a secondary commitment-enacting force."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "hearer",
      "analysis": "The nearby reference to caressing suggests that the hearer receives the exchange affectionately, although the passage does not provide his verbal interpretation of the statement."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "narrator_or_text",
      "analysis": "The text embeds the statement in sustained, reflective direct speech rather than presenting it as an uncontrolled outburst. Its causal syntax makes it an explanation of the speaker's happiness and hopes."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "reader",
      "analysis": "A reader can naturally construe the words primarily as a sincere avowal and secondarily as affirming commitment to the anticipated shared and married life."
    }
  ],
  "features": [
    "quoted",
    "mixed_reading"
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "text": "I think it a glorious thing to have the hope of living with you, because I love you.",
      "supports": "The love claim functions propositionally as the stated cause of the speaker's hope and happiness, strongly supporting the truth-conditional reading. The prospect of living together also gives it some commitment-related force."
    },
    {
      "text": "No, sir, don’t caress me now—let me talk undisturbed.",
      "supports": "The speaker deliberately continues an organized account, which weighs against treating the occurrence as a largely reflexive or merely exclamatory production. The caress indicates an affectionate interpersonal setting."
    },
    {
      "text": "I thought of the life that lay before me—your life, sir",
      "supports": "The speaker imagines her future as joined to the hearer's, supporting a secondary performative construal of the avowal as affirming relational commitment."
    },
    {
      "text": "Sophie called me upstairs to look at my wedding-dress",
      "supports": "The anticipated wedding supplies a conventional commitment context, though the love statement itself remains an explanatory avowal rather than an explicit promise or vow."
    },
    {
      "text": "thinking of you; and I beheld you in imagination so near me, I scarcely missed your actual presence",
      "supports": "The extended description of affectionate preoccupation corroborates the asserted emotional state and gives no textual indication of deception or manipulation."
    }
  ],
  "needs_more_context": true,
  "context_request": "The hearer's subsequent response and additional surrounding dialogue would help determine how he construes the avowal and whether it materially renews or undertakes commitment. Wider immediate context could also test sincerity or strategic use, though neither is placed in doubt here.",
  "typology_adequate": true,
  "typology_diagnosis": "The typology gives a natural account: this is primarily truth-conditional, with a weaker performative dimension arising from the shared-future and wedding context. The controlled causal explanation provides essentially no support for an exclamatory/reflexive reading.",
